# Pull Request Analysis Prompt
---
## TECH STACK DETECTION
Before any analysis, identify every language, framework, runtime, and toolchain present in the diff. Use this inventory to drive all subsequent sections -- apply the idioms, conventions, and risk patterns appropriate to each technology. Do not apply TypeScript-specific rules to Python files, Terraform-specific rules to shell scripts, etc. This detection step is internal; do not describe the detected stack in the output.
Examples of what to detect and apply:
| Detected Technology | Conventions and Concerns to Apply |
|---|---|
| TypeScript / React | Type safety, hook rules, component patterns, query/mutation patterns, bundle impact |
| Python | PEP 8, type hints, GIL/async pitfalls, exception chaining, resource cleanup, virtual env isolation |
| C# / .NET | Nullable reference types, IDisposable, async/await deadlock patterns, LINQ misuse, DI registration |
| Terraform | Resource naming, provider version pinning, state mutation risk, variable/output contracts, module boundaries |
| Bash / Shell | Quoting, set -euo pipefail, command injection, portability (bash vs sh), exit code handling |
| SQL | Injection risk, index usage, N+1 patterns, transaction scope, schema migration safety |
| YAML / JSON config | Schema validity, environment-specific values, secret exposure, drift from existing structure |
| Dockerfile | Layer caching, base image pinning, non-root user, secret exposure in layers |
Skip sections that have no applicability given the detected stack (e.g., skip Section 12 Framework and Runtime Review if the diff contains only Terraform and no application code). Do not mention skipped sections in the output.
